<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors>CS8600;CS8602;CS8603;CS8618;CS8625</WarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      The proxy interfaces mirror D-Bus contracts rather than being an API surface we document
      member by member, and the prose comments deliberately explain the parameters that are
      surprising instead of listing every one. CS1591 = missing doc, CS1573 = partially documented.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    These bind Linux system daemons. They build anywhere (useful for CI on windows-latest and for
    editor tooling), but they only do anything on a machine with a D-Bus system bus.
  -->
  <!--
    IsTrimmable is deliberately NOT set, even though these libraries are aimed at embedded
    consumers that would like to trim.

    Tmds.DBus builds its proxies reflectively: CreateProxy<IFooProxy> generates an implementation
    at runtime from the interface's type and member names, and the [Dictionary] property classes
    are populated by matching field names against D-Bus dictionary keys. Nothing in that chain is
    visible to the trimmer as a use of those names, so a trimmed consumer can lose them and every
    call then fails at runtime. IsTrimmable would advertise a guarantee this dependency cannot
    honour.

    Worse, it advertises it silently: Tmds.DBus 0.92.0 carries no RequiresUnreferencedCode
    annotations on that path, so the trim analyzer has nothing to warn about and the claim looks
    verified. The analyzer stays on below to catch anything that IS annotated, but a clean build
    is not evidence these assemblies survive trimming.

    Tmds.DBus.Protocol is documented as NativeAOT- and trim-compatible, and its source generator
    emits proxies of the same shape as Dbus/Proxies.cs. Moving to it is the route to a trimmable
    build; until then, do not re-add IsTrimmable.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Package metadata">
    <Authors>Rafael Vuijk</Authors>
    <Product>Linux D-Bus Clients for .NET</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) Rafael Vuijk</Copyright>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Rafael-SOWNet/linux-dbus-clients</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Rafael-SOWNet/linux-dbus-clients</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>dbus;linux;networkmanager;modemmanager;avahi;mdns;dns-sd;zeroconf;embedded</PackageTags>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <!-- Set by CI from the git tag; 0.1.0-dev locally. -->
    <Version Condition="'$(Version)'==''">0.1.0-dev</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)'!='false'">
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    SourceLink only matters for the packages CI publishes, so it is opt-in via
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild (which the workflow passes explicitly) rather than always on.

    Keeping it unconditional caused real trouble: these projects are also consumed as a git
    submodule of a downstream repository, cloned from a mirror on a different, self-hosted git
    host. Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub does not recognise that host, so every downstream build
    emitted "Source control information is not available" once per project. Registering the
    mirror's host here would fix the warning but would hard-code a private hostname into a
    public repository, which §8 forbids. Scoping SourceLink to CI solves both.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)'=='true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" Version="8.0.0" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
